<!-- This vignette is precomputed: edit school-zones.Rmd.orig and run
vignettes/precompile.R. It calls the live API, which CRAN builds can't. -->

Georgia Civic Data publishes school attendance zones: the areas that assign
a home to its zoned elementary, middle and high school. This article maps
one district's zones, checks which schools have no zone and how precise the
zones are, and finds the places a location falls in.

```{r setup, message = FALSE}
library(georgiacivicdata)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
```

The geometry functions return 'sf' objects, so install 'sf' to map them.
Every one also takes `geometry = FALSE` for a plain tibble.

## Map one district's zones

```{r zones}
zones <- gcd_school_zones(district = "Atlanta Public Schools")
zones |>
  sf::st_drop_geometry() |>
  count(zone_level, method, accuracy_class)
```

```{r map, fig.height = 3.4, fig.alt = "Map of Atlanta Public Schools elementary, middle and high school attendance zones.", fig.cap = "Atlanta Public Schools zones by level; each colour is a school."}
ggplot(zones) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = school_name), colour = "white", linewidth = 0.2,
          show.legend = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~ factor(zone_level, levels = c("elementary", "middle", "high"))) +
  labs(title = "Atlanta Public Schools attendance zones, 2026-27") +
  theme_void()
```

The default `resolution = "display"` returns simplified polygons for maps.
`resolution = "full"` returns the source polygons, which are much larger;
filter to a district before asking for them.

## Schools without a zone

Charter, magnet, alternative and virtual schools have no attendance zone.
`gcd_school_zone_status()` says which schools have one and why the others
don't:

```{r status}
status <- gcd_school_zone_status(district = "Atlanta Public Schools")
count(status, zone_status, no_zone_category)
attr(status, "note")
```

## How precise are the zones?

Zones come from very different sources. Some are the district's official GIS
layers; others are digitized from PDF maps, rebuilt from street lists, or
are the whole district outline (in districts with one school per grade).
Every zone says how it was built (`method`) and how exact it is
(`accuracy_class`); treat them accordingly.

```{r precision}
coverage <- gcd_school_zone_coverage()
coverage |>
  count(zone_level, level_status) |>
  tidyr::pivot_wider(names_from = zone_level, values_from = n)
```

`gcd_school_zone_coverage()` gives one row per district and level, with the
share of the district's land and 2020 population its zones cover, and the
reason for anything short of complete:

```{r coverage}
coverage |>
  filter(district_name == "Appling County") |>
  select(zone_level, level_status, pct_population, methods, reason)
```

## Locate a place

`gcd_locate()` takes points (`lat`, `lon`) or street addresses and returns
the county, Census tract and block group, school district and zoned schools
each falls in, one row per place. These are public landmarks:

```{r locate-points}
landmarks <- tibble::tribble(
  ~landmark,                      ~lat,     ~lon,
  "Georgia State Capitol",        33.7490,  -84.3880,
  "Stone Mountain Park",          33.8053,  -84.1455,
  "Okefenokee Swamp Park",        31.0558,  -82.2701,
  "Appling County Courthouse",    31.7782,  -82.3482
)
places <- gcd_locate(lat = landmarks$lat, lon = landmarks$lon,
                     id = landmarks$landmark)
places |>
  select(id, geographic_type, geographic_id, geographic_name, grades)
```

The output is long: filter it to the rows you need, such as
`filter(places, geographic_type == "high_school")`. A school's
`geographic_id` is its `"DISTRICT-SCHOOL"` code, the form `gcd_rows()` takes
as `school_id`.

The `note` column explains any row that is missing or needs care, such as a
level with no zone collected yet:

```{r notes}
noted <- filter(places, !is.na(note))
noted |> select(id, geographic_type, geographic_name)
writeLines(strwrap(noted$note, width = 76))
```

### Addresses

Addresses are geocoded on your computer with the U.S. Census batch
geocoder; only the resulting coordinates are sent to Georgia Civic Data.
Give one-line addresses or a data frame with `street`, `city`, `state` and
`zip`:

```{r address}
capitol <- gcd_locate(address = "206 Washington St SW, Atlanta, GA 30334")
capitol |>
  select(match_status, matched_address, geographic_type, geographic_name, grades)
```

### Split grade runs

Some homes are zoned to one elementary school for the early grades and
another for the later ones. Then a level has more than one school row, and
`grades` tells them apart. Oakhurst Elementary in the City Schools of
Decatur teaches kindergarten to grade 2; locating the school itself shows
both of its neighborhood's elementary zones:

```{r split}
oakhurst <- gcd_dimension("schools", district_code = "773", q = "Oakhurst",
                          active = TRUE,
                          columns = c("school_name", "latitude", "longitude"))
oakhurst

split <- gcd_locate(lat = oakhurst$latitude, lon = oakhurst$longitude)
split |>
  filter(endsWith(geographic_type, "_school")) |>
  select(geographic_type, geographic_id, geographic_name, grades)
```

`grade` keeps only the school for one grade:

```{r grade}
gcd_locate(lat = oakhurst$latitude, lon = oakhurst$longitude, grade = "4") |>
  filter(endsWith(geographic_type, "_school")) |>
  select(geographic_type, geographic_name, grades)
```

### The default assignment

A zone is the default assignment, not the only option:

```{r assignment}
attr(capitol, "assignment_note")
```

Students can attend another public school by transfer, or a charter, magnet
or other choice school, and these zones are not an official enrollment
determination: confirm an assignment with the school district.
